# Searching with Vim's patterns.

=== slash finds the next match
text:
|foo bar foo baz
keys: /foo<CR>
want:
foo bar |foo baz

=== n and N
text:
|x a x a x
keys: /x<CR>nN
want:
x a |x a x

=== search wraps around
text:
a x |b
c
keys: /x<CR>
want:
a |x b
c

=== question mark searches backwards
text:
foo bar foo |baz
keys: ?foo<CR>
want:
foo bar |foo baz

=== star searches the word under the cursor
text:
|foo foobar foo
keys: *
want:
foo foobar |foo

=== hash searches it backwards
text:
foo foobar |foo
keys: #
want:
|foo foobar foo

=== word boundaries
text:
|ab xab ab
keys: /\<ab\><CR>
want:
ab xab |ab

=== magic groups and alternation
text:
|cat dog bird
keys: /\(dog\|bird\)<CR>n
want:
cat dog |bird

=== quantifiers
text:
|ax aax aaax
keys: /a\{3}x<CR>
want:
ax aax |aaax

=== very magic
text:
|a1 b22 c333
keys: /\v[a-z]\d{3}<CR>
want:
a1 b22 |c333

=== zs and ze
text:
|foobar bazbar
keys: /baz\zsbar<CR>
want:
foobar baz|bar

=== ignore case with \c
text:
|x FOO
keys: /\cfoo<CR>
want:
x |FOO

=== a count with n
text:
|1 a 2 a 3 a
keys: /a<CR>2n
want:
1 a 2 a 3 |a

=== a pattern not found is an error and the cursor stays
text:
a|bc
keys: /zzz<CR>
want:
a|bc
error: yes

=== backslash classes in Vim's ASCII meaning
text:
|ab 12 cd
keys: /\d\d<CR>
want:
ab |12 cd

=== search in the visual mode extends the selection
text:
|abc def ghi
keys: v/gh<CR>d
want:
|hi
